{
  "image_name": "0e34a16e96206cb99b5c38f877513c28.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure outlines how DNA polymerase theta (Polθ) may repair double-strand breaks through theta-mediated end joining. At the top, Polθ is shown as a multi-domain protein with helicase-like and polymerase regions. The central schematic summarizes the repair pathway: broken DNA ends are first recognized, then brought together while short matching sequences (microhomologies) are searched. Non-matching 3′ tails are trimmed, and Polθ-driven DNA synthesis extends one end using the other as a template. The process can finish through either a short-patch or long-patch route, involving ligation and possibly flap removal, with several accessory factors shown as proposed contributors.\n\nThe lower part compares two synthesis modes. In intermolecular synthesis, one DNA end copies sequence from the other end, promoting repair products with direct-repeat templated insertions. In intramolecular synthesis, a single end folds back on itself to prime DNA synthesis, which can be unproductive or generate inverted-repeat insertions before switching into productive joining. The diagrams emphasize that Polθ’s helicase and polymerase activities appear to cooperate during end alignment, synthesis, and resolution, while some steps and factors remain uncertain, as indicated by question marks and alternative labels.",
